/* =====================================================================
   Conclusion Lab question bank (Year 12, 7138).
   For each question the student gets two finished paragraphs and writes
   only the conclusion. On a 9-mark Assess question the paragraphs argue
   for and against; on a 15-mark Evaluate question they make the case for
   each option (or each way).

   Where the questions come from (field "from"):
   - sam: the 7138 sample assessment materials (Paper 1). The case
     information is from the AQA insert and the notes from the AQA mark scheme.
   - adapted: a 7132 past paper question rewritten as a 7138 9-mark Assess
     question. The figures are from the original paper.
   - homework: the Year 12 homework questions in hw-bank.js (made-up
     businesses). These stay locked in the lab until the homework is due
     ("open"), so nobody sees the paragraphs before writing the answer.

   Every paragraph names and uses at least one model or theory from the
   7138 specification. Run CBANK.modelCheck() in the console to list them.
   No student data here.
   ===================================================================== */
(function(){
"use strict";

/* what each kind of question needs from a conclusion */
const TYPES={
  a9:{name:'9-mark Assess',marks:9,levels:3,
    tip:'Make a clear decision on the exact question. Say which side carries more weight for this business and why, and what your answer depends on.',
    marker:'7138 9-mark Assess question on a case study (Levels 1–3). AQA: "The question requires a decision on this particular issue." For the top level "there must be a clear answer to the question set which has a weighing up of an argument and counter argument", with "evaluation which effectively weighs up the arguments made and reaches a convincing and coherent judgement, and fully addresses the question". Level 2 has "evaluation which has some support" but "may not weigh up the different arguments made". Level 1 is "evaluation based on assertion and without support". A judgement may be qualified ("it depends on…") as long as there is a clear answer. The judgement should be about this business in its situation, using the case.'},
  a9q:{name:'9-mark Assess with a calculation',marks:9,levels:3,
    tip:'Make a clear decision on the exact question and use the result of the calculation in it. Say what the figure shows, why it matters more (or less) than the evidence on the other side, and what your answer depends on.',
    marker:'7138 9-mark Assess question with a quantitative focus (Levels 1–3). AQA Level 3: "evaluation which effectively weighs up the arguments made and uses the calculation findings to reach a convincing and coherent judgement, and fully addresses the question". Level 3 answers compare the calculated figure with other information in the case and "may consider the limitations of the data". Level 2: "evaluation which has some support and may be based on the calculation findings" but may not weigh up the arguments. Level 1: judgement "based on assertions" and "not derived from numerical analysis or the context of the business". In a conclusion, quoting the figure without saying what it shows is weaker than using it to decide the question.'},
  e15o:{name:'15-mark Evaluate: two options',marks:15,levels:5,
    tip:'Recommend one of the two options. Don’t just say which has more advantages. Say why its strengths matter more for this business, given its situation and what it is trying to achieve, and what the decision depends on.',
    marker:'7138 15-mark Evaluate question where the two options are given (Levels 1–5). AQA Level 5: "evaluation is well supported and weighs up both options, provides a coherent overarching judgement and fully addresses the question set"; it "will consider the context of the business situation before making an appropriate, well-supported recommendation" and "is likely to consider the highest priority to the business given the context, for example, short-term, long-term returns". Level 4: "supported, has some balance of the arguments and has a focus on the question" but without the clear focus on the business’s priorities. Level 3: "some support but may lack balance of arguments and is not fully focused on the question". "If the judgement does not address all the issues in the question or leaves out some of the issues it has raised (for example, the counter argument) this is unlikely to demonstrate the qualities of a top level of response." Where the question has a calculation, a top conclusion uses the calculated figures in the recommendation.'},
  e15w:{name:'15-mark Evaluate: two ways',marks:15,levels:5,
    tip:'Recommend one of the two ways in the paragraphs. Say why it suits this business better, given its situation and what it is trying to achieve, and what the decision depends on.',
    marker:'7138 15-mark Evaluate question where the student identifies two ways to solve a problem and recommends one (Levels 1–5). Here the two ways are the ones in the two paragraphs. AQA Level 5: "evaluation is well supported and weighs up both options, provides a coherent overarching judgement and fully addresses the question set", considering "the highest priority to the business given the context, for example, short-term, long-term returns". Level 4: supported, some balance, focused on the question, but less focus on the business’s priorities. Level 3: some support but may lack balance or focus. A recommendation must say which one is most suitable, not that both should be done. Where the question has a calculation, a top conclusion uses the calculated figures in the recommendation.'}
};

const HW_DUE={1:'2026-12-09',2:'2026-12-16',3:'2027-01-13',4:'2027-01-20'};
const hw=w=>({from:'homework',src:`Year 12 homework, week ${w}`,open:HW_DUE[w]});

const Q=[
/* ============================ 7138 SAMPLE PAPER 1 ============================ */
{id:'S1-01.3',type:'a9',from:'sam',src:'7138 sample Paper 1, Q01.3',
 q:'Assess whether increasing spending on influencers would lead to higher profits for Gymshark Ltd.',
 facts:['Gymshark Ltd sells gymwear and is a hit with younger gym goers in a highly competitive market. Its competitors include Nike, Adidas and Puma.','Instead of traditional advertising or high street shops, it has grown by having athletes and social media influencers wear its clothes on Instagram, TikTok and Facebook. Ben Francis, the founder, still shares his own fitness videos to promote the brand.','The company has 18 million followers on social media.','Appendix C (2024): Facebook 3.0 billion monthly users (largest age group 25–34); Instagram 2.0 billion (25–34); TikTok 1.6 billion (18–24).','Ben wants Gymshark to be the UK equivalent of Nike.'],
 forP:`On one hand, spending more on influencers could lead to higher profits because it reaches Gymshark's target market cheaply. Gymshark's customers are younger gym goers, and TikTok's biggest age group is 18–24, so influencers who post there put the brand in front of exactly the people most likely to buy it. Seeing athletes they follow wearing the clothes makes the brand stand out from Nike and Adidas, which is Porter's differentiation strategy. Customers who feel loyal to a differentiated brand are less sensitive to price, so demand becomes more price inelastic and Gymshark can keep its prices high rather than discounting. This means sales volume rises without the profit per item falling. Influencer posts are also cheaper than TV or billboard adverts for the number of people they reach, so if the extra revenue is bigger than the extra fees, profit increases. Gymshark has grown this way from a garage to a business worth over $1 billion, which suggests the approach works for it.`,
 againstP:`However, more influencer spending will not necessarily raise profits, because Gymshark may get less back from each extra pound. Gymshark already has 18 million followers, so many of the people extra influencers reach will already know the brand. Each extra post may bring in fewer new customers than the last. At the same time, rivalry in sportswear is intense (Porter's Five Forces), and Nike, Adidas and Puma also pay influencers. This competition pushes up the fees that popular influencers charge, so costs rise while extra sales get smaller. There is also a risk to the brand. If an influencer behaves badly, customers link that behaviour to Gymshark, sales fall and the money spent is wasted. Ben's own videos already promote the brand for free, so paying outsiders more may add cost without adding much revenue. As a result, the extra fees could grow faster than the extra gross profit, so profit could fall.`,
 depends:['whether the extra revenue from each pound spent is more than the extra cost (the return on the spending)','how carefully influencers are chosen and how the risk of a scandal is controlled, for example through contracts','how many potential customers have not yet heard of Gymshark (has the reach already been used up?)','what rivals spend on the same influencers, which pushes fees up'],
 report:'The mark scheme says a top answer must judge the effect on profit, not just weigh up the general pros and cons of influencers. It suggests the judgement depends on the cost of the influencers compared with the extra revenue, and on how well influencers are chosen and the risks controlled.',
 reportHead:'From the AQA mark scheme',
 model:`Overall, spending more on influencers is likely to increase Gymshark's profits, but only if each extra pound brings in more than a pound of extra gross profit. Influencers are how the brand was built, and TikTok reaches the 18–24-year-olds who are its main customers, so the money is going where its buyers are. The risk is that Nike and Adidas are paying for the same influencers, so fees go up while the extra sales shrink, because many people already follow Gymshark. So the main thing it depends on is the return on the extra spending, which Gymshark can track post by post. It also depends on picking influencers carefully, because one scandal could undo a lot of good work. If Gymshark measures the return and keeps using Ben's own free videos, more spending should mean higher profits.`},

{id:'S1-02.3',type:'a9',from:'sam',src:'7138 sample Paper 1, Q02.3',
 q:'Assess whether an increase in spending on market research could be profitable for Apple.',
 facts:['In September 2023 Apple launched the Vision Pro, a virtual reality headset controlled by hand movements, voice and eye tracking. Analysts estimated over $8 billion had been spent developing it over five years.','Apple reportedly hoped to sell 3 million in the first year. Some experts expected sales below 200,000, lower than any other major Apple launch.','The price was $3,499, three times Meta’s Quest headset. The battery lasted only two hours and some users said the headset was awkward to wear.','Meta had been selling the Quest at a loss to increase its market share.','Steve Jobs once said customers do not know what they want until Apple makes it.','Over 2 billion Apple devices are in use. Apple has a huge database of information about its users, a wide product portfolio and a trusted brand: in a 2021 survey three times as many people said they would buy a headset from Apple as from its closest competitor.'],
 forP:`On one hand, spending more on market research could be profitable because it would help Apple avoid expensive mistakes like the Vision Pro. Apple spent over $8 billion developing it but may sell fewer than 200,000 in the first year against a hope of 3 million. Research such as product testing and focus groups with potential buyers could have shown that a $3,499 price, a two-hour battery and an awkward headset would put people off. Apple could then have changed the product or the price before launch. Virtual reality is a new market for Apple, so the Vision Pro is close to diversification in Ansoff's Matrix, the riskiest strategy, because Apple has less knowledge of these customers. Research reduces that risk. Even a large increase in research spending is tiny compared with $8 billion, so if it stops one failed launch, or gets the marketing mix right so more units sell, the extra revenue will easily cover its cost and profit will rise.`,
 againstP:`However, more spending on market research might not be profitable for Apple because it already knows a lot about its customers. With over 2 billion devices in use and a huge database of user information, Apple can see how people use its products without paying for new research, so extra research may tell it little it does not already know. Research also takes time. Rivalry in the headset market is intense (Porter's Five Forces), with Meta selling the Quest at a loss to win market share, so if Apple delays launches to do more research, Meta gains customers first and Apple's future sales are lower. Apple's success has often come from products customers did not know they wanted, as Steve Jobs said, so asking customers may not predict what will sell. Its trusted brand also means people are three times more likely to buy an Apple headset than a rival's. As a result, the extra research could add cost and delay without increasing revenue, which would reduce profit.`,
 depends:['what kind of research it is and how good the sample is (an unrepresentative sample is of little value)','how new the product or market is: research matters most where Apple has least knowledge','how much the research slows down launches in a fast-moving market','how much the extra research costs compared with the size of the decision it informs'],
 report:'The mark scheme says it would depend on what the research was and how it was conducted, for example an unrepresentative sample would be of limited value. Apple already has a lot of data, but the Vision Pro shows the cost of getting it wrong. For the very top of the level there must be a clear sense of an increase in spending.',
 reportHead:'From the AQA mark scheme',
 model:`I think more spending on market research could be profitable for Apple, but only for products in markets it does not yet know well. For its existing products, Apple's database from 2 billion devices already tells it what customers do, so extra research would mostly add cost and slow it down against Meta. The Vision Pro is different, because virtual reality was a new market and the $8 billion was spent without finding out whether people would pay $3,499 for a headset with a two-hour battery. So it depends mainly on how new the product is, and also on the research being done properly with a representative sample. Spending more is profitable if it is aimed at risky launches like the Vision Pro, where one avoided mistake is worth billions.`},

{id:'S1-02.4',type:'a9',from:'sam',src:'7138 sample Paper 1, Q02.4',
 q:'Assess whether a large investment in new product development is likely to increase Apple’s market share.',
 facts:['Appendix D: Apple’s investment in new product development was $21,914 million in 2021 and $29,915 million in 2023. Sales were $365,817 million in 2021 and $394,328 million in 2023.','Appendix F shows that iPhone sales grew quickly for many years but growth has slowed recently.','Over $8 billion was spent developing the Vision Pro. Apple hoped to sell 3 million in the first year; some experts expected fewer than 200,000.','The Vision Pro was priced at $3,499, three times Meta’s Quest. Meta had been selling the Quest at a loss to increase its market share.','Apple has a wide product portfolio, a trusted brand and over 2 billion devices in use.'],
 forP:`On one hand, a large investment in new product development is likely to increase Apple's market share because its markets move fast and products do not last forever. Appendix F shows that iPhone sales growth has slowed, which suggests it is in the maturity stage of the product life cycle. Without new products, Apple's sales would eventually fall as rivals bring out better ones. Apple raised its spending on new product development from $21.9 billion to $29.9 billion between 2021 and 2023, an increase of 36.5%. New products sold to Apple's existing 2 billion users are product development in Ansoff's Matrix, which is lower risk than diversification because these customers already trust the brand. New features keep Apple's products different from rivals' (Porter's differentiation strategy), so customers stay with Apple instead of switching. This means Apple's sales grow faster than the market, so its market share increases.`,
 againstP:`However, a large investment does not guarantee a higher market share, as the Vision Pro shows. Apple spent over $8 billion developing it, but experts expected fewer than 200,000 sales in the first year, far below the 3 million Apple hoped for. In the Boston Matrix it looks like a question mark: a product in a new market where Apple's share is low and may stay low. The main reason is the price. At $3,499 it costs three times as much as Meta's Quest, and Meta is selling the Quest at a loss to win market share, so for most buyers demand for an Apple headset is likely to be price elastic. Spending more on development does not change that. New products can also take sales from Apple's existing ones rather than from rivals, so Apple's total sales rise less than it hoped. This means market share depends on the price and the rest of the marketing mix, not just on how much is invested.`,
 depends:['how well the money is used: much new product development spending is wasted on products that fail or never launch','what rivals are doing (if Meta and others keep launching, Apple has to keep up)','the time period: in the short term Apple can rely on its existing portfolio and extension strategies','the price and the rest of the marketing mix of the new products'],
 report:'The mark scheme says new product development is almost certainly essential in a technology market in the long term, but what matters is how well the money is used, not just how much is spent. For the top mark the answer must be about market share, not just sales.',
 reportHead:'From the AQA mark scheme',
 model:`Overall, a large investment in new product development is likely to increase Apple's market share in the long run, but not every product it pays for will do so. The iPhone is in the maturity stage and rivals like Meta keep launching, so without new products Apple's share would slowly fall. But the Vision Pro shows that spending $8 billion does not guarantee sales if the price is three times a rival's. So the result depends mainly on how well the money is used, especially getting the price and features right for what customers will pay. It also depends on the time period, because in the short term Apple's existing products protect its share anyway. So the investment should increase market share over time, as long as Apple spends it on products customers want at a price they will pay.`},

/* ===================== 7132 QUESTIONS REWRITTEN FOR 7138 ===================== */
{id:'A-2022-01.5',type:'a9',from:'adapted',src:'Adapted from 7132 Paper 2, June 2022, Q01.5',
 q:'Assess whether now is a good time to launch a new brand of tea in the UK.',
 facts:['Tea drinking in the UK is declining as younger customers choose coffee. Extrapolation suggests coffee will outsell tea by 2025.','A "health tea" niche (fruit, herbal and green teas) is growing, helped by its link with wellbeing.','UK black tea sales fell from about 96 million kg (2012) to about 68 million kg (2020).','2019 sales: Twinings £108m (+3.8%), PG Tips £99m (−7.2%), Taylors £98m (+5.0%), Tetley £90m (+0.4%), Pukka £23m (+15.4%, organic and ethical), other brands £144m.','Price elasticity of demand: black tea −1.2, health teas −0.5.','28% of 18–24-year-olds never drink black tea (16% of over-65s). 32% of women and 19% of men drink health tea. 62% say ethical sourcing is important.'],
 forP:`On one hand, now could be a good time to launch a new tea brand if it is aimed at the health tea niche. Pukka, which sells organic and ethical teas, grew its sales by 15.4% in 2019, much faster than any of the top four brands, so demand for this type of tea is growing. Health teas have a price elasticity of −0.5, which is inelastic, so a new brand could charge a premium price and a 10% price rise would only cut sales volume by about 5%, meaning revenue would rise. Customers want this kind of product too, as 62% say ethical sourcing matters and 32% of women already drink health tea. Aiming a differentiated product at one niche rather than the whole market is Porter's focus strategy, so the new brand would not have to compete head-on with PG Tips and Tetley. This means it could win customers who are not loyal to the big brands and earn a good profit margin on each box.`,
 againstP:`However, now is not a good time to launch a brand in the tea market as a whole, because it is shrinking. Black tea sales fell from about 96 million kg in 2012 to about 68 million kg in 2020, a fall of about 29%, and coffee is expected to overtake tea. Young people are moving away from tea, with 28% of 18–24-year-olds never drinking black tea, so the market is likely to keep shrinking as older drinkers are replaced. The top four brands also have about 70% of the market (£395m out of £562m), and their strong brands act as a barrier to entry (Porter's Five Forces). Black tea has a price elasticity of −1.2, so a new brand could not charge a premium and would have to compete on price against firms with much bigger economies of scale. As a result, a new black tea brand would struggle to win enough sales to cover its launch costs and make a profit.`,
 depends:['which segment the new brand targets: the growing health and ethical niche or the shrinking black tea market','what makes it different from the big four, and how it is positioned against them','whether it has the money to promote the brand and get into supermarkets','whether the trends in the data carry on'],
 report:'Adapted from a 7132 question. The examiner report said the best answers focused on whether "now" is the right time, linked different parts of the data and used their own calculations, such as the top four brands’ 70% share. Both yes and no judgements were rewarded when they were supported.',
 reportHead:'From the examiners (original 7132 question)',
 model:`Now is a good time to launch a new brand of tea only if it is a health or ethical tea, not a traditional black tea. The market as a whole is shrinking, with black tea down about 29% since 2012 and the top four brands holding around 70% of sales, so a new black tea would be fighting for a smaller market against strong brands. The health tea niche is going the other way: Pukka grew by 15.4%, and demand is inelastic at −0.5, so a new brand could charge a premium and still sell. So the answer depends mainly on which segment the brand targets. It also depends on having enough money to get onto supermarket shelves next to established names. Overall, now is a good time for a health tea brand, but not for a black tea brand.`},

{id:'A-2024-01.4',type:'a9',from:'adapted',src:'Adapted from 7132 Paper 2, June 2024, Q01.4',
 q:'Biggs Ltd is launching a new range of snacks. Assess whether sponsoring a major televised sporting competition is a good way for Biggs Ltd to promote the new range.',
 facts:['Biggs Ltd is one of six firms that dominate the UK snack market, which has grown strongly for ten years, largely because of new snacks for children. Biggs’s market share has been falling since 2019. Potato prices have risen 35% in two years.','The competition attracts a wide range of ages and is popular with families.','Revenue £47.6m (2023), £47.0m (2022). Operating profit £4.65m (2023), £4.70m (2022). Gross profit margin fell from 48.6% to 46.9%.','Marketing spend as % of revenue in 2023: Biggs 10.7%, average of the six largest firms 18.4%, market leader 24%. Biggs’s forecast for 2024 with the sponsorship: 27.4%.','Interviews with 27 families in 2021 (scores out of 10). "I will definitely try the new products": age 6–16: 9.9, 17–35: 7.2, 36+: 1.8. "The campaign improves my view of the brand": 8.3, 6.3, 8.0.','Independent survey of 3,000 people. Share of snack purchases: 6–16: 19%, 17–35: 28%, 36+: 53%. Share of snacks eaten: 46%, 43%, 11%.'],
 forP:`On one hand, the sponsorship is a good way to promote the new range because it reaches the people who eat the most snacks. 6–16-year-olds eat 46% of all snacks and scored 9.9 out of 10 for definitely trying the new products. Because the competition is popular with families, the sponsorship puts the range in front of children, who can then ask their parents to buy it, and parents scored 8.0 for the campaign improving their view of the brand. It also deals with Biggs's main weakness. In 2023 it spent only 10.7% of revenue on marketing compared with 18.4% for the six largest firms, which helps explain why its market share has fallen since 2019. With six big firms fighting for the same shoppers, rivalry is intense (Porter's Five Forces), so a firm that promotes less than its rivals keeps losing share. A new range sold to existing customers is product development in Ansoff's Matrix, and it needs strong promotion to get noticed, so the sponsorship should increase sales and win back market share.`,
 againstP:`However, the sponsorship may not be a good way to promote the range because of its cost and the weak evidence behind it. If revenue stays at about £47.6m, spending 27.4% on marketing means about £13m, compared with about £5.1m in 2023, so roughly £8m extra. That is far more than Biggs's operating profit of £4.65m, at a time when its gross margin has fallen from 48.6% to 46.9% because potato prices rose 35%, a sign of strong supplier power (Porter's Five Forces). The over-36s make 53% of purchases but scored only 1.8 for trying the products, so children's enthusiasm may not turn into sales. The interviews were with only 27 families in 2021, so they may not represent buyers today. As a result, Biggs could spend £8m and not get enough extra sales to cover it, turning its profit into a loss.`,
 depends:['whether children’s enthusiasm leads parents, who make 53% of purchases, to buy','whether Biggs can afford about £8m of extra spending when its operating profit is £4.65m','how far the 2021 research with 27 families can be trusted','how rivals respond and how popular the competition turns out to be'],
 report:'Adapted from a 7132 question. The mark scheme said a new promotional strategy was needed and this one seemed well targeted, but success would depend on competitors’ actions, the accuracy of the research, the popularity of the event and whether the reward justified the large financial risk.',
 reportHead:'From the mark scheme (original 7132 question)',
 model:`Overall, sponsorship is a good way to reach the right people, but at this cost it is a risky way to promote the range. Biggs clearly needs to promote more, as it spent 10.7% of revenue on marketing against 18.4% for the biggest firms, and the competition reaches 6–16-year-olds, who eat 46% of snacks and scored 9.9 for trying the range. The problem is that about £8m of extra spending is nearly double last year's operating profit. Whether it works depends mainly on whether parents buy what their children want, because the over-36s make 53% of purchases but scored only 1.8. It also depends on whether research from just 27 families in 2021 can be trusted. So it is a good choice only if Biggs tests the idea first and is confident parents will follow their children.`},

/* ============================ YEAR 12 HOMEWORK ============================ */
Object.assign(hw(1),{id:'hw01-9a',type:'a9',caseId:'kiln',
 q:'Assess whether Kiln & Co Ltd should use the results of its customer survey to decide whether to open a pop-up shop.',
 forP:`On one hand, Priya should use the survey because it gives her fairly reliable evidence before a big decision. 400 customers replied and 62% said they would visit a Kiln & Co shop. At a 95% confidence level the interval is ±4.8 percentage points, so the true figure is between 57.2% and 66.8%. Even at the bottom of that range, over half of customers say they would visit. The pop-up costs about £38,000, which is 13% of Kiln & Co's 2025 revenue of £296,000, so it is a large risk for a small business, and the survey lowers that risk for very little cost because it was emailed. The people asked are customers who already pay £24 for a handmade mug. They value something they cannot get from Dunelm (Porter's differentiation strategy), so they are the most likely to buy in a shop. This means Priya can use the survey to judge whether the shop will bring in enough revenue to cover the £38,000.`,
 againstP:`However, Priya should not use the survey to decide, because the sample does not match the people who would actually use a shop in Leicester. Around 70% of website customers live outside the East Midlands, so most of the 400 people who replied could not reach a Leicester shop within 30 minutes. The 62% only tells Priya how many would visit a shop near them, not this shop. At most about 30% of the sample, roughly 120 people, are likely to be local. Saying you would visit is also not the same as buying. A city centre shop needs new local customers walking past, which is market development in Ansoff's Matrix, and the survey says nothing about them because it only asked people who already buy online. As a result, the survey is likely to overstate demand, and Priya could spend £38,000 on a shop that does not get enough sales to cover the rent.`,
 depends:['how many of the people who replied actually live near Leicester (about 70% of customers live outside the East Midlands)','whether the pop-up needs new local shoppers or existing customers, and whether the survey tells Priya anything about the first group','whether Priya can add other evidence, such as footfall figures or a trial at a market stall','how big the £38,000 risk is compared with Kiln & Co’s revenue'],
 report:'A strong answer uses the confidence interval properly (57.2% to 66.8%) but then questions who was asked. The key point in the case is that 70% of customers live outside the East Midlands, so the survey tells Priya little about a Leicester shop.',
 reportHead:'What a strong answer weighs up',
 model:`Priya should not use the survey on its own to decide. The result looks strong, because even the bottom of the confidence interval is 57.2%, but most of the people asked could never get to a Leicester shop, since 70% of customers live outside the East Midlands. The survey measures whether customers like the idea of a shop near them, not whether enough people in Leicester will walk in and buy. That matters more than the size of the sample, because a sample of 400 is no use if it is the wrong 400. So it depends mainly on how many of the replies came from people near Leicester. Priya could look at only the local replies and add some footfall research before risking £38,000, which is 13% of her revenue.`}),

Object.assign(hw(1),{id:'hw01-9b',type:'a9q',caseId:'northbank',
 q:'Assess whether Northbank Coffee plc’s shares are still a good investment for its shareholders.\nCalculate the dividend yield for Northbank Coffee plc in January 2026 and use it in your answer.',
 calc:'Dividend per share 2025 = £20m ÷ 250m shares = 8p. Dividend yield January 2026 = 8p ÷ 228p × 100 = 3.51%. (For comparison, January 2025: 12p ÷ 310p = 3.87%.)',
 forP:`On one hand, Northbank's shares could still be a good investment. The dividend per share in 2025 was £20m ÷ 250m = 8p, so at a price of 228p the dividend yield in January 2026 is 8 ÷ 228 × 100 = 3.51%. Shareholders still get a reasonable income every year. The share price has also fallen by 26% from 310p, so the shares are now cheap. The fall was caused by higher wage and energy costs, which may only be temporary. If profits recover, more investors will want to buy the shares, so the price rises and shareholders who buy now make a capital gain. Northbank also has a clear point of difference. Its Fairtrade coffee and "Good coffee, fairly bought" message are Porter's differentiation strategy, and 34% of customers say ethical sourcing affects where they buy coffee. Loyal customers like these should protect its sales across 640 shops, so its profits and dividends should recover.`,
 againstP:`However, Northbank's shares may no longer be a good investment, because both parts of the return have fallen. The dividend was cut from £30m to £20m, so the dividend per share fell from 12p to 8p. The yield fell from 3.87% to 3.51%, even though the share price also fell, which would normally push the yield up. Shareholders who bought at 310p have lost 82p a share, a fall of 26%. The causes may not be temporary. The National Living Wage keeps rising and the staff forum is already asking for a pay rise, so costs are likely to keep going up. Rivalry in coffee shops is intense (Porter's Five Forces), with Costa, Starbucks, Pret and Greggs all nearby, so Northbank cannot easily raise prices to cover the extra costs without losing customers. As a result, its profit margins are likely to stay squeezed, which means the dividend may be cut again and the share price may keep falling.`,
 depends:['whether the shareholder wants a regular income or a rise in the share price','whether the rise in wage and energy costs is temporary or will keep going','how the 3.51% yield compares with what investors could earn elsewhere','whether Northbank can pass higher costs on to customers given the competition'],
 report:'The calculation is 8p ÷ 228p = 3.51%. A strong answer says what the figure shows: the yield has fallen even though the share price fell, because the dividend was cut by a third. It then judges whether the fall in costs and profit is likely to be temporary.',
 reportHead:'What a strong answer weighs up',
 model:`Overall, Northbank's shares are still a reasonable investment for shareholders who want income, but not a good one for those wanting growth in the short term. A yield of 3.51% still gives a steady income, but it has fallen from 3.87% even though the share price dropped by 26%, which shows how much the dividend cut has hurt. The key question is whether the higher costs are temporary. Energy costs may come down, but wages are likely to keep rising, and with Costa, Starbucks and Greggs competing for the same customers, Northbank cannot simply raise its prices. So it depends mainly on whether Northbank can get its costs under control. Until it shows that, the shares are worth holding for the income but not a good time to buy more.`}),

Object.assign(hw(1),{id:'hw01-15a',type:'e15o',caseId:'northbank',labels:['Switch to cheaper beans','Keep Fairtrade and hold the dividend'],
 q:'Northbank Coffee plc needs to improve its profit. It is choosing between:\n• switching to cheaper coffee beans that are not Fairtrade certified\n• keeping its Fairtrade beans and holding the dividend at £20 million for another year.\nEvaluate these two options and recommend which Northbank Coffee plc should choose.',
 forP:`Switching to cheaper beans would improve Northbank's profit quickly and by a known amount. The finance director has found it would save about £6 million a year, and because this is a cut in cost of sales, gross profit rises straight away by the same amount. Northbank's profit fell because wage and energy costs went up, and this is one of the few costs it can control. It would also please its most powerful stakeholders. Large shareholders want the dividend restored to £30 million, and in stakeholder mapping they have high power and high interest, so they must be managed closely. If they sell their shares the price falls further and it becomes harder to raise finance. The £6 million would cover more than half of the £10 million needed to restore the dividend. Most customers do not seem to mind about sourcing, since 66% of those surveyed did not say it affects where they buy, so most of Northbank's sales should be safe while costs fall.`,
 againstP:`Keeping the Fairtrade beans and holding the dividend protects the thing that makes Northbank different. Its adverts say "Good coffee, fairly bought", so its ethical sourcing is its main point of difference from Costa, Starbucks and Greggs (Porter's differentiation strategy). 34% of customers say ethical sourcing affects where they buy coffee. If Northbank switched, many of them would feel misled and could easily move to a rival, because switching costs in coffee shops are almost zero and rivalry is intense (Porter's Five Forces). Across 640 shops, losing even a small share of these customers could cost more than the £6 million saved. Under Carroll's CSR pyramid, Fairtrade is an ethical responsibility, and dropping it could cause bad publicity that damages the brand for years. Holding the dividend at £20 million also keeps £10 million a year in the business, which could pay for the barista pay rise or efficiency savings that improve profit without risking the brand.`,
 depends:['how many of the 34% of customers who care about ethical sourcing would actually switch to a rival, and how much revenue that would cost compared with the £6m saving','whether the business’s priority is short-term profit for shareholders or long-term brand value','how much power the large shareholders have and how they would react to the dividend staying at £20m','whether the retained £10m is actually used to improve profit'],
 report:'Neither option is clearly right. Cheaper beans give a certain £6m saving now; keeping Fairtrade protects the brand that 34% of customers care about. A strong recommendation explains which matters more for Northbank and notes that holding the dividend does not raise profit by itself unless the retained money is used well.',
 reportHead:'What a strong answer weighs up',
 model:`I recommend that Northbank keeps its Fairtrade beans and holds the dividend at £20 million. The £6 million saving from cheaper beans is certain, but it would undo the one thing that makes Northbank different from Costa and Starbucks, as its whole brand is "Good coffee, fairly bought". With 34% of customers saying ethical sourcing matters and switching between coffee shops so easy, losing even a few percent of sales across 640 shops could wipe out the saving, and the damage would last longer than one year. The decision depends mainly on how many of those customers would actually leave, which Northbank could test before deciding. Holding the dividend does not raise profit on its own, so it only works if the £10 million kept is spent on things like staff pay and efficiency. Large shareholders will be unhappy, but protecting the brand gives the best chance of higher profit in the long term.`}),

Object.assign(hw(1),{id:'hw01-15b',type:'e15w',caseId:'kiln',labels:['Sell in new markets','New products for existing customers'],
 q:'Priya wants Kiln & Co Ltd’s sales to keep growing faster than the market.\nEvaluate two ways Kiln & Co Ltd could do this and recommend which is most suitable.\nCalculate the growth of the UK online handmade homeware market and Kiln & Co’s sales growth between 2024 and 2025 and use them in your answer.',
 calc:'Market growth 2024–25 = (£410m − £365m) ÷ £365m × 100 = 12.3%. Kiln & Co sales growth = (£296,000 − £231,000) ÷ £231,000 × 100 = 28.1%.',
 forP:`One way is for Kiln & Co to sell its existing range in new markets, for example to independent gift shops around the UK or through a pop-up shop. This is market development in Ansoff's Matrix. Kiln & Co's sales grew by 28.1% between 2024 and 2025 ((£296,000 − £231,000) ÷ £231,000), more than twice the market's 12.3% ((£410m − £365m) ÷ £365m). But most of that growth came from online customers, and sales growth usually slows as a small business uses up its first group of buyers. Kiln & Co has only about 0.07% of a £410 million market, so there are many potential customers who have never seen its products. Gift shops would put the mugs in front of people who do not shop on Etsy or Priya's website, and each shop orders in bulk, so sales would rise quickly. The products are already tested, so there is less risk than developing new ones.`,
 againstP:`Another way is to develop new products for existing customers, such as matching dinner sets or personalised mugs, which is product development in Ansoff's Matrix. Kiln & Co already has a base of loyal website customers who pay £24 for a handmade mug, so they value its design and quality (Porter's differentiation strategy). Selling them more items means each customer spends more, and Priya can reach them cheaply by email, as she did with her survey. This keeps sales growing faster than the market's 12.3% without the cost of finding new customers or giving a margin to gift shops, which would take a share of the price. Priya's background is in marketing and she is good at testing new designs, as she showed when she fixed the cracked glazes. New products also protect Kiln & Co's niche against Dunelm and H&M Home, which cannot easily copy handmade designs. As a result, revenue rises at a high profit margin.`,
 depends:['whether Kiln & Co has the capacity to make more, as it is a handmade business with four employees','whether the loyal website customers will buy more items, or whether growth needs new customers','how much of the price Kiln & Co would have to give gift shops, and whether it can still make a profit','how much risk Priya is willing to take, and how much money she can invest'],
 report:'Kiln & Co’s sales grew 28.1% against market growth of 12.3%. A strong recommendation uses both figures and points out that keeping growth that far ahead of the market gets harder as the business grows, so the choice depends on where the next customers will come from and whether Kiln & Co can make enough.',
 reportHead:'What a strong answer weighs up',
 model:`I recommend developing new products for existing customers. Kiln & Co grew by 28.1% last year, more than twice the market's 12.3%, and that growth came from loyal online customers who are happy to pay £24 for a handmade mug. Selling them dinner sets or personalised pieces keeps sales growing at a high margin and can be done cheaply by email, whereas gift shops would want a cut of the price. Selling in new markets would reach more people, but it is a bigger step for a business with only four staff. The main thing it depends on is capacity. Both options mean making more by hand, and new products can be launched in small batches while bulk orders from shops might be more than Priya can make. If demand from existing customers starts to slow, she can then move into new markets.`}),

Object.assign(hw(2),{id:'hw02-9a',type:'a9q',caseId:'fernhill',
 q:'Assess whether Fernhill Foods Ltd should raise the price of the Fernhill Kitchen range from £4.50 to £4.95.\nCalculate the expected change in revenue from the Fernhill Kitchen range and use it in your answer.',
 calc:'Price rise = 10%. PED −0.6, so quantity falls by 6%: 2.4m × 0.94 = 2.256m packs. Revenue now = 2.4m × £4.50 = £10.8m. New revenue = 2.256m × £4.95 = £11,167,200. Change = +£367,200 (+3.4%). If real incomes also fall 2% with YED +1.6, quantity falls a further 3.2%, to about 2.184m packs, so revenue is about £10.81m, roughly unchanged.',
 forP:`On one hand, Fernhill should raise the price because demand for Fernhill Kitchen is price inelastic. The price rise from £4.50 to £4.95 is 10%. With a PED of −0.6, sales volume should fall by only 6%, from 2.4 million to 2.256 million packs. Revenue would rise from £10.8 million (2.4m × £4.50) to £11.17 million (2.256m × £4.95), an increase of £367,200. Fernhill Kitchen is a premium brand, so its customers are buying taste and quality rather than the cheapest meal (Porter's differentiation strategy), which is why they are not very sensitive to price. Fernhill also makes 144,000 fewer packs, so its total variable costs fall at the same time as revenue rises. The extra money covers the rising ingredient costs, so the price rise should protect the range's profit margin instead of letting costs squeeze it.`,
 againstP:`However, the price rise may not increase revenue next year because real incomes are expected to fall by 2%. Fernhill Kitchen has an income elasticity of +1.6, so it is a luxury, and a 2% fall in income would cut demand by about 3.2% on top of the 6% fall from the price rise. Sales would fall to about 2.18 million packs, and revenue would be about £10.81 million, almost exactly what it is now. When incomes fall, some customers trade down to cheaper meals, and Fernhill's own-label range, with a YED of −0.4, shows that value meals gain from this. Supermarkets' own premium ranges are close substitutes (Porter's Five Forces), so a higher price makes it easier for customers to switch. The PED of −0.6 is also only an estimate from an agency, and it may become more elastic when money is tight. As a result, Fernhill could raise its price and still see no rise in revenue, while losing customers it may not win back.`,
 depends:['how accurate the agency’s PED estimate of −0.6 is, and whether demand becomes more elastic when incomes fall','how much real incomes actually fall (YED +1.6 means the range is hit hard by a fall)','how much ingredient costs have risen: even with flat revenue, fewer packs means lower total costs','how rivals and the supermarkets’ own premium ranges price their meals'],
 report:'The calculation shows revenue rising by £367,200 (2.4m × £4.50 = £10.8m; 2.256m × £4.95 = £11.17m). A strong answer uses this but also brings in the YED of +1.6: with real incomes falling 2%, revenue is likely to stay roughly the same. It can still argue for the rise because fewer packs means lower costs.',
 reportHead:'What a strong answer weighs up',
 model:`Fernhill should raise the price to £4.95, but it should not expect the £367,200 rise in revenue that the PED suggests. With a PED of −0.6, a 10% price rise only cuts sales by 6%, so on its own it increases revenue. The bigger issue is the 2% fall in real incomes, because with a YED of +1.6 that cuts demand by another 3.2%, leaving revenue at about £10.8 million, the same as now. Even so, Fernhill would be making about 216,000 fewer packs for the same revenue, so its costs fall and its profit should be higher, which matters because ingredient costs are rising. It depends mainly on whether the PED estimate is right when people have less money to spend. If customers start trading down to own-label meals, the price rise could lose more sales than expected.`}),

Object.assign(hw(2),{id:'hw02-9b',type:'a9',caseId:'volta',
 q:'Assess whether raising brand awareness is the right marketing objective for Volta Bikes Ltd in 2026.',
 forP:`On one hand, raising brand awareness is the right objective because Volta's target customers mostly do not know it exists. Only 12% of commuters in its target cities have heard of Volta, so 88% could never consider buying one. Commuters are the biggest segment, at 46% of the 210,000 e-bikes sold in 2025, which is 96,600 bikes, and they already buy 80% of Volta's bikes. Volta sold 7,350 bikes, so it has only 3.5% of the market and there is plenty of room to grow. Selling more of an existing product to an existing segment is market penetration in Ansoff's Matrix, the lowest-risk way to grow. An e-bike costing £1,850 is a big purchase, so people research brands carefully, and a brand they have heard of is more likely to make their shortlist. The objective is also SMART, rising from 12% to 20% by December, so Volta can measure whether its spending works. Higher awareness should lead to more test rides and more sales.`,
 againstP:`However, brand awareness may not be the right objective because it does not guarantee sales. People can know the name and still buy a rival bike, so a better objective might be conversion, such as turning more test rides into sales, which links directly to revenue. Volta's 2025 marketing already returned £930,000 of profit from £600,000 of spending, so its current approach seems to work and the problem may not be awareness. The objective also keeps Volta focused only on commuters. Leisure riders are 38% of the market (79,800 bikes) and delivery riders 16% (33,600), and Jess and Tom both want to grow in these segments. If commuters are where rivalry is fiercest (Porter's Five Forces), spending to raise awareness there may cost more than it brings in. As a result, an awareness objective could use up the marketing budget without increasing Volta's sales or profit.`,
 depends:['whether low awareness is really what is holding back sales, or whether Volta loses customers later, for example after test rides','whether Volta plans to keep focusing on commuters or move into leisure or delivery riders','how competitive the commuter segment is and how much it costs to reach people there','whether higher awareness can be measured and linked to sales'],
 report:'A strong answer uses the 12% figure and the size of the commuter segment, but asks whether awareness is the problem holding Volta back. The case also shows Jess and Tom want to grow in other segments, which could make an objective aimed only at commuters the wrong one.',
 reportHead:'What a strong answer weighs up',
 model:`Overall, raising brand awareness is the right objective for Volta in 2026, as long as it stays focused on commuters. With only 12% of commuters in its target cities aware of the brand, most potential buyers cannot choose Volta, and commuters are both the biggest segment and 80% of its sales. Conversion matters too, but you cannot convert people who have never heard of you. The weakness is that awareness does not pay the bills on its own. So it depends mainly on whether low awareness is really what is holding sales back, and Volta should check this by tracking how many new test rides turn into sales. If the company decides to move into leisure or delivery riders, the objective would need to change, but for 2026 it fits.`}),

Object.assign(hw(2),{id:'hw02-15a',type:'e15o',caseId:'volta',labels:['Cargo e-bike for delivery riders','Leisure riders with the existing range'],
 q:'Volta Bikes Ltd wants to grow. It is choosing between:\n• launching a cargo e-bike for delivery riders\n• targeting leisure riders with its existing range.\nEvaluate these two options and recommend which Volta Bikes Ltd should choose.\nCalculate the size of the delivery rider and leisure rider segments in units and use them in your answer.',
 calc:'Delivery riders = 16% × 210,000 = 33,600 units. Leisure riders = 38% × 210,000 = 79,800 units. (Commuters 46% = 96,600.) Volta sold 7,350 bikes, 3.5% of the market.',
 forP:`Launching a cargo e-bike for delivery riders could help Volta grow quickly because each customer buys a lot of bikes. The delivery segment is 16% of 210,000, which is 33,600 bikes a year. Delivery companies buy in bulk, so winning one contract could bring hundreds of sales at once, and Volta would need only a small sales team rather than large consumer adverts. If Volta won 5% of the segment, that would be 1,680 bikes, 23% more than the 7,350 it sold in 2025. Delivery riders use their bikes all year, so demand is not seasonal, which helps Volta plan production. A tough, easy-to-repair bike would give Volta a clear point of difference in this segment (Porter's differentiation strategy). Bulk buyers have strong buyer power (Porter's Five Forces) and will push the price down, but if Volta designs the bike to be cheap to repair it can sell on lower running costs rather than a lower price, protecting its profit margin.`,
 againstP:`Targeting leisure riders with its existing range is the bigger and safer opportunity. The leisure segment is 38% of 210,000, which is 79,800 bikes, more than twice the 33,600 delivery bikes. If Volta won 5% of leisure riders, that would be 3,990 extra bikes, 54% more than it sold in 2025. Selling existing bikes to a new segment is market development in Ansoff's Matrix, which is less risky than the cargo bike, a new product for a new market, which is diversification. Volta would not need to design and test a new bike. It already knows how to sell to individual riders through social media and test-ride events, and its marketing returned £930,000 of profit from £600,000 in 2025. The weakness is that leisure riders mostly buy in spring and summer, so sales would be seasonal, and the lower-priced model would earn less profit per bike. But the much larger segment means total profit should still rise.`,
 depends:['how much Volta can afford to spend designing a new cargo bike compared with new adverts and a cheaper model','how much the lower-priced leisure model and the delivery companies’ buying power would cut the profit made on each bike','whether Volta can cope with leisure riders buying mostly in spring and summer','how much risk Volta is willing to take, and whether its priority is quick growth or steady growth'],
 report:'The calculations give 33,600 delivery and 79,800 leisure bikes. A strong recommendation uses the size of the segments and links each option to Ansoff (market development is lower risk than diversification), then weighs that against seasonality and the profit per bike.',
 reportHead:'What a strong answer weighs up',
 model:`I recommend that Volta targets leisure riders with its existing range. The leisure segment is 79,800 bikes, more than twice the 33,600 bought by delivery riders, so even a small share of it would add more sales than a good share of the delivery market. It is also the lower-risk choice: Volta keeps selling the bikes it already makes, using marketing it knows works, whereas the cargo bike means designing a new product for buyers it has never sold to. The cargo bike's advantages are bulk orders and demand all year, but delivery firms would use their buying power to push the price down. The decision depends mainly on how much the lower-priced model reduces profit per bike, and on Volta planning production and cash flow around a spring and summer peak. If it can manage that, leisure riders offer more growth for less risk.`}),

Object.assign(hw(2),{id:'hw02-15b',type:'e15w',caseId:'fernhill',labels:['More own-label value meals','A lower-priced Fernhill Kitchen range'],
 q:'Real incomes are expected to fall next year.\nEvaluate two ways Fernhill Foods Ltd could protect its revenue and recommend which is most suitable.',
 forP:`One way to protect revenue is for Fernhill to make more own-label value meals for the supermarkets. The own-label range has an income elasticity of −0.4, so it is an inferior good. If real incomes fall by 2%, demand for value meals should rise by about 0.8%, as shoppers trade down from more expensive meals. Supermarkets will want more value meals on their shelves, so Fernhill can offer to take on bigger contracts or add more own-label lines. It already has the factory and the relationships with two supermarkets, so it can increase output quickly and spread its fixed costs over more meals, lowering unit costs. This shifts Fernhill's sales towards the part of the market that grows when incomes fall, so total revenue is protected even if Fernhill Kitchen sales drop. Because supermarket shoppers are also more price sensitive when money is tight, a value range they already trust is well placed to win the sales they move away from premium meals.`,
 againstP:`Another way is to launch a lower-priced line under the Fernhill Kitchen brand, so customers who want to spend less can trade down without leaving Fernhill. Fernhill Kitchen has an income elasticity of +1.6, so a 2% fall in real incomes could cut its sales by about 3.2%. A cheaper line keeps those shoppers buying a brand they already trust. This is product development in Ansoff's Matrix, a new product for existing customers in the same supermarkets, so the risk is fairly low. It also reduces Fernhill's dependence on own-label work, where the supermarkets have strong buyer power (Porter's Five Forces) and review prices every year. Selling more own-label would make that dependence worse, as it is already 55% of revenue. With the cheaper line, Fernhill sets its own prices and keeps the brand loyalty it has built, so revenue is protected without handing even more power to the supermarkets.`,
 depends:['how far real incomes actually fall, and for how long','whether the supermarkets want more own-label meals from Fernhill and at what price, given their buying power','whether a cheaper Fernhill Kitchen line would take sales from the existing premium range instead of from rivals','whether Fernhill’s priority is revenue next year or reducing its long-term dependence on two supermarkets'],
 report:'A strong answer uses the two YED figures: the premium range (+1.6) loses sales when incomes fall and own-label (−0.4) gains. It then weighs the quick gain from own-label against the risk of depending even more on two powerful buyers.',
 reportHead:'What a strong answer weighs up',
 model:`I recommend making more own-label value meals, at least for next year. The YED figures point that way: own-label demand should rise by about 0.8% as incomes fall, while Fernhill Kitchen could lose about 3.2%. Fernhill already has the factory and the supermarket contracts, so it can grow own-label output quickly, whereas a new cheaper line takes time to develop and might just take sales from the premium range. The danger is that own-label is already 55% of revenue, and the supermarkets review prices every year. So the decision depends mainly on whether the supermarkets will give Fernhill extra orders at a price it can live with. Because the fall in incomes is expected to be short-term, own-label is the better way to protect revenue next year, but Fernhill should not let it grow much beyond where it is.`}),

Object.assign(hw(3),{id:'hw03-9a',type:'a9q',caseId:'brightwave',
 q:'Assess whether Brightwave Toys Ltd should stop selling Word Wizard.\nCalculate the percentage change in sales of Word Wizard between 2024 and 2025 and use it in your answer.',
 calc:'Percentage change = (£0.4m − £0.55m) ÷ £0.55m × 100 = −27.3%.',
 forP:`On one hand, Brightwave should stop selling Word Wizard because it is in decline. Its sales fell from £0.55 million in 2024 to £0.4 million in 2025, a fall of 27.3%, which is much faster than its market, which shrank by 4%. That means Word Wizard is losing customers to rivals as well as suffering from a declining market, a sign it is in the decline stage of the product life cycle. In the Boston Matrix it is a dog: low market growth (−4%) and a tiny market share of 2%, against 19% for the largest rival. Products like this usually make little profit and are unlikely to recover. The money, warehouse space and marketing time spent on it have an opportunity cost, because they could be used for Glow Track, a star with 18% market growth, or for launching Bolt. Stopping Word Wizard would let Brightwave focus its resources on products with a future, which should increase its overall profit.`,
 againstP:`However, Brightwave should not necessarily stop selling Word Wizard, because it still brings in £0.4 million of revenue. The Boston Matrix only looks at market share and market growth, not profit, so it does not tell us whether Word Wizard still makes money. If it sells for more than it costs to make, it makes a contribution towards Brightwave's fixed costs. Dropping it would mean the other products have to cover those costs, so profit could fall. The market is only shrinking by 4% a year, so there are still buyers. An extension strategy, such as a new version or a lower price before Christmas, could slow the decline (product life cycle). Toy shops and supermarkets may also want Brightwave to offer a range of games, not just blocks and racing sets. As a result, keeping Word Wizard could be more profitable than stopping it, at least until it stops covering its own costs.`,
 depends:['whether Word Wizard still covers its own costs and makes a contribution to fixed costs','whether the money and space it uses would earn more if put into Glow Track or Bolt (opportunity cost)','whether an extension strategy could slow the 27.3% fall','whether retailers expect Brightwave to offer a full range'],
 report:'Sales fell 27.3%, much faster than the market (−4%), and Word Wizard is a dog in the Boston Matrix. A strong answer then points out that the Boston Matrix does not show profit, so the decision depends on whether the game still makes a contribution and what else the resources could be used for.',
 reportHead:'What a strong answer weighs up',
 model:`Brightwave should stop selling Word Wizard, unless it can show the game still makes a worthwhile contribution. A 27.3% fall in one year is far faster than the 4% fall in its market, so customers are moving to rivals, and with a 2% share against a rival's 19% it has little chance of recovering. The case for keeping it is that £0.4 million of sales may still cover its costs, and the Boston Matrix says nothing about profit. But the resources it uses have a clear opportunity cost now that Brightwave has Glow Track growing at 18% and Bolt to launch. So the decision depends mainly on whether Word Wizard is still profitable. If it is barely covering its costs, the money and shelf space would earn more behind Bolt and Glow Track.`}),

Object.assign(hw(3),{id:'hw03-9b',type:'a9',caseId:'brightwave',
 q:'Assess whether developing Bolt is likely to improve Brightwave Toys Ltd’s competitiveness.',
 forP:`On one hand, developing Bolt is likely to improve Brightwave's competitiveness because its current best seller has stopped growing. Stackers has been the best seller for twelve years, but sales have not grown since 2022 and its market is growing by only 1%. It is a cash cow in the Boston Matrix and is in the maturity stage of the product life cycle. Brightwave needs new products to replace it. Bolt is a programmable robot, so it gives Brightwave something different from its wooden blocks and from many rivals (Porter's differentiation strategy). Because it is new, Brightwave can charge more and win customers looking for something educational. Bolt will be sold through Brightwave's existing toy shops, supermarkets and website, so this is product development in Ansoff's Matrix, which uses channels and customers it already has. This should increase sales and market share, making Brightwave more competitive against bigger toy companies.`,
 againstP:`However, Bolt may not improve Brightwave's competitiveness for long. Market research suggests rivals could launch similar robots within 18 months. There is nothing in the case to stop them copying it, so barriers to entry are low (Porter's Five Forces), and once copies appear Brightwave's advantage disappears and it may have to cut its price. Bigger toy and technology firms already have experience with app-controlled toys, so they may be able to make robots more cheaply or with better apps. Brightwave is a family firm known for wooden toys, so it may not have the skills to keep updating the app and fixing problems, and poor reviews would damage the brand. It has already spent £1.4 million developing Bolt. If sales are disappointing, that money cannot be used to support Glow Track, its star. As a result, Bolt could give Brightwave only a short-term boost, or even make it less competitive if it fails.`,
 depends:['how quickly rivals copy Bolt, and whether Brightwave can protect or keep improving it','whether Brightwave has the skills to support an app-based product','how Bolt is priced and promoted at launch','whether the £1.4m would have been better spent supporting Glow Track'],
 report:'A strong answer uses the fact that Stackers has stopped growing to show why a new product is needed, and the 18-month warning to judge how long any advantage will last. "Competitiveness" means more than sales: it is about whether Brightwave can compete better against rivals.',
 reportHead:'What a strong answer weighs up',
 model:`Developing Bolt is likely to improve Brightwave's competitiveness, but probably only for a limited time unless it keeps developing the product. Stackers has stopped growing, so Brightwave needs a new product to compete with, and Bolt gives it something different that can be sold through the shops and supermarkets it already uses. The weakness is the 18-month warning, because once rivals launch similar robots the advantage starts to disappear. So it depends mainly on whether Brightwave can stay ahead after that, for example by updating the app and adding new robots, which is hard for a firm used to making wooden toys. If it can, Bolt should become a star and make Brightwave more competitive. If it cannot, it will give a short boost and then struggle against bigger rivals.`}),

Object.assign(hw(3),{id:'hw03-15a',type:'e15o',caseId:'brightwave',labels:['Price skimming at £79.99','Penetration pricing at £44.99'],
 q:'Brightwave Toys Ltd is choosing between:\n• price skimming at £79.99\n• penetration pricing at £44.99\nfor the launch of Bolt.\nEvaluate these two options and recommend which Brightwave Toys Ltd should choose.\nCalculate the expected first-year revenue for each option and use them in your answer.',
 calc:'Skimming revenue = 22,000 × £79.99 = £1,759,780. Penetration revenue = 48,000 × £44.99 = £2,159,520. Contribution: skimming (£79.99 − £31) × 22,000 = £1,077,780; penetration (£44.99 − £31) × 48,000 = £671,520.',
 forP:`Price skimming at £79.99 would earn Brightwave more profit in the first year, even though revenue is lower. Expected revenue is 22,000 × £79.99 = £1,759,780. Each robot costs £31 to make and deliver, so each one sold makes £48.99, a total contribution of £1,077,780. At £44.99 each robot makes only £13.99, so penetration pricing gives £671,520, about £406,000 less, even though it sells more than twice as many. Skimming recovers 77% of the £1.4 million development cost in the first year, against 48% for penetration. Bolt is new and different (Porter's differentiation strategy), and parents who want the latest educational toy are less sensitive to price at first, so a high price fits. Rivals are not expected for 18 months, so Brightwave can earn high margins while it has the market to itself, then lower the price when copies arrive.`,
 againstP:`Penetration pricing at £44.99 would bring in more revenue and build a bigger customer base before rivals arrive. Expected revenue is 48,000 × £44.99 = £2,159,520, about £400,000 more than skimming. Selling 48,000 robots means more than twice as many children using Bolt and its app, so more parents and friends see it, and word of mouth increases future sales. If rivals launch similar robots within 18 months, the business with the most customers will be hardest to catch, and a low price makes entry less attractive for rivals (Porter's Five Forces). A lower price also suits supermarkets, one of Brightwave's main channels, where toys are often bought on impulse and shoppers compare prices. Winning a large share early could turn Bolt into a star in the Boston Matrix, so Brightwave gains more in later years even though it makes less profit at the start.`,
 depends:['whether Brightwave’s priority is recovering the £1.4m and making profit now, or winning market share before rivals arrive','how quickly rivals really launch, and whether Brightwave could lower the price once they do','how reliable the sales forecasts of 22,000 and 48,000 are','which channel sells most Bolts: specialist toy shops (less price sensitive) or supermarkets'],
 report:'Revenue: skimming £1,759,780, penetration £2,159,520. A strong answer goes further and notices that skimming makes more profit per robot, so contribution is higher (about £1.08m against £0.67m). The recommendation then depends on whether Brightwave values profit now or market share before rivals arrive in 18 months.',
 reportHead:'What a strong answer weighs up',
 model:`I recommend price skimming at £79.99. Penetration pricing brings in about £400,000 more revenue (£2,159,520 against £1,759,780), but because each robot costs £31, skimming makes about £406,000 more contribution in the first year and recovers 77% of the £1.4 million spent developing Bolt. Bolt has no direct rivals for about 18 months, so this is the time to charge a high price to parents who want something new. The main risk is that rivals arrive with a bigger customer base to fight against, which is what penetration pricing would give Brightwave. So the decision depends mainly on how soon rivals launch. If it really is 18 months, Brightwave can skim first and lower the price when copies appear, which gives it both the profit now and a cheaper Bolt later.`}),

Object.assign(hw(3),{id:'hw03-15b',type:'e15o',caseId:'fernhill',labels:['Cost-based pricing','Competition-based pricing'],
 q:'Fernhill Foods Ltd is choosing between:\n• cost-based pricing\n• competition-based pricing\nfor its new plant-based range.\nEvaluate these two options and recommend which Fernhill Foods Ltd should choose.',
 forP:`Cost-based pricing would make sure Fernhill makes a profit on every meal and could help it win sales. Each meal costs £2.80 to make, so with Fernhill's usual 40% mark-up the price would be £2.80 × 1.4 = £3.92. That gives £1.12 profit on every meal sold. £3.92 is below every rival, whose similar meals sell for £4.25 to £4.75, so Fernhill would be the cheapest plant-based meal on the shelf. Plant-based meals are a newer product for many shoppers, and a lower price encourages them to try it, so Fernhill could gain market share quickly. Competing on low price is closer to Porter's cost leadership strategy. It is also simple, because Fernhill already prices this way, and if ingredient costs rise the price can rise with them, so the margin is always protected. With real incomes expected to fall by 2%, a cheaper option could also appeal to shoppers who are cutting back.`,
 againstP:`Competition-based pricing, setting a price within the £4.25 to £4.75 range that rivals charge, would earn Fernhill much more on each meal. At £4.50, each meal makes £1.70 over its £2.80 cost, 52% more than the £1.12 it makes at £3.92. Customers are clearly willing to pay these prices, as rivals already sell at them. Market mapping shows that plant-based meals are positioned as a mid-to-premium product, and the Fernhill Kitchen brand has a PED of −0.6, which suggests its customers are not very price sensitive. If Fernhill priced well below rivals, it might be seen as lower quality and give up profit it could have earned. It might also start a price war with rivals, and rivalry between ready meal brands is already strong (Porter's Five Forces). Pricing alongside rivals lets Fernhill compete on taste and brand instead of price, so its profit margins are much higher.`,
 depends:['how price sensitive plant-based shoppers are (the PED of the new range is not known)','whether the range is sold under the Fernhill Kitchen brand, where customers are less price sensitive','whether Fernhill’s priority is winning market share quickly or making a high profit margin','how rivals would react to being undercut'],
 report:'Cost-based pricing gives £3.92 (£2.80 × 1.4), below all rivals; competition-based pricing at around £4.50 earns £1.70 a meal instead of £1.12. A strong recommendation explains which matters more for Fernhill: market share or margin. It also points out that the PED of the new range is not given, so any judgement about price sensitivity is an assumption.',
 reportHead:'What a strong answer weighs up',
 model:`I recommend competition-based pricing at around £4.50. Cost-based pricing gives a price of £3.92, which is cheaper than every rival but means Fernhill makes £1.12 on each meal instead of £1.70, so it would be giving away over a third of its margin to customers who already pay £4.25 to £4.75. A very low price could also make the range look cheap next to Fernhill Kitchen. The case for £3.92 is that it could win share quickly, especially as incomes fall. So the decision depends mainly on how price sensitive plant-based shoppers are, which we do not know. If they buy mainly on taste and brand, as Fernhill Kitchen's customers seem to, pricing in line with rivals gives Fernhill much more profit for very few lost sales.`}),

Object.assign(hw(4),{id:'hw04-9a',type:'a9q',caseId:'volta',
 q:'Assess whether Volta Bikes Ltd’s marketing spending in 2025 was successful.\nCalculate the return on marketing spend for 2025 and use it in your answer.',
 calc:'Return on marketing spend = £930,000 ÷ £600,000 = 1.55 (155%): £1.55 of profit for every £1 spent.',
 forP:`On one hand, Volta's marketing spending in 2025 was successful because it paid for itself and more. The return on marketing spend is £930,000 ÷ £600,000 = 1.55, so every £1 spent on marketing generated £1.55 of profit. This means the £600,000 was not just a cost but added £930,000 of profit to the business. The spending was mostly social media adverts and test-ride events, which target commuters, the segment that buys 80% of Volta's bikes. Test rides let people feel how light and simple the bike is, which is what makes it different from rivals (Porter's differentiation strategy), so they are more likely to buy at £1,850. Volta sold 7,350 bikes, about 3.5% of the 210,000 sold in the UK, which is a good share for a business started in 2020. This shows that the marketing helped Volta grow its sales profitably.`,
 againstP:`However, we cannot be sure the spending was successful, because the £930,000 is Volta's own estimate. It is very hard to know how many sales were caused by the marketing and how many would have happened anyway as more people buy e-bikes. Volta may have counted every sale after a test ride, even if the customer had already decided to buy. There is also nothing to compare the 1.55 with, such as Volta's return in previous years or rivals' returns. And after all this spending, only 12% of commuters in Volta's target cities have heard of the brand. If the aim was to build the brand, the spending has had limited effect. A business growing through market penetration in Ansoff's Matrix needs its existing segment to know about it, so low awareness suggests the marketing is not reaching enough people. As a result, the spending may have been less successful than the 1.55 figure suggests.`,
 depends:['how reliable Volta’s estimate of £930,000 of profit from marketing is','what the 1.55 is compared with (previous years, rivals, other uses of the money)','what the spending was meant to achieve, given that awareness is still only 12%','whether the profit came from new customers or ones who would have bought anyway'],
 report:'The calculation is £930,000 ÷ £600,000 = 1.55. A strong answer says what this means (£1.55 of profit for every £1 spent) and then questions the data: the profit figure is Volta’s own estimate, there is nothing to compare it with, and awareness is still only 12%.',
 reportHead:'What a strong answer weighs up',
 model:`Overall, Volta's marketing spending in 2025 looks successful, but not as clearly as the 1.55 suggests. On Volta's figures, every £1 spent brought in £1.55 of profit, so the spending more than paid for itself. However, the £930,000 is Volta's own estimate, and in a growing market some of those customers would probably have bought anyway. The 12% awareness figure also suggests the adverts are not reaching most commuters. So the judgement depends mainly on how reliable that profit estimate is. If Volta measured it carefully, for example by tracking which customers came from test rides, the spending was a success. If not, the most we can say is that it probably covered its cost.`}),

Object.assign(hw(4),{id:'hw04-9b',type:'a9',caseId:'fernhill',
 q:'Assess whether Fernhill Foods Ltd should continue to make own-label meals for supermarkets.',
 forP:`On one hand, Fernhill should continue to make own-label meals because they are 55% of its revenue. Losing more than half its revenue would leave the factory running well below capacity. Fixed costs like rent and machinery would then be spread over far fewer meals, so the unit cost of every meal, including Fernhill Kitchen, would rise. Making large volumes for supermarkets gives Fernhill economies of scale, especially when buying ingredients. Own-label meals also protect Fernhill when the economy is weak. They have an income elasticity of −0.4, so if real incomes fall by 2% as expected, demand rises by about 0.8% while Fernhill Kitchen, at +1.6, falls. Having one product that does well when incomes fall and one that does well when they rise spreads Fernhill's risk across its portfolio. This means continuing with own-label keeps Fernhill's revenue and profit steadier.`,
 againstP:`However, Fernhill may be better off moving away from own-label because the supermarkets have so much power over it. Two supermarkets buy 55% of Fernhill's output, review the price every year and have threatened to move to other suppliers. This is strong buyer power (Porter's Five Forces), so they can keep pushing Fernhill's price down and its profit margin on these meals is likely to be low. Own-label demand also has a PED of −1.8, which shows customers buy on price, so Fernhill cannot get the supermarkets to accept higher prices when its costs rise. Every hour the factory spends on own-label meals has an opportunity cost, because it could be making Fernhill Kitchen meals, which have a PED of −0.6 and can be sold at a premium. If Fernhill moved its capacity towards its own brand over time, it would earn more on each meal and depend less on two powerful customers.`,
 depends:['how much profit Fernhill actually makes on own-label meals compared with Fernhill Kitchen','whether there is enough demand for Fernhill Kitchen to fill the capacity used by own-label','how likely the supermarkets are to switch suppliers or cut the price again','the economy: own-label does better when real incomes fall'],
 report:'A strong answer uses the 55% figure and the two YED values to show why own-label matters, then weighs this against the supermarkets’ buyer power and the low margins it causes. "Continue" does not have to mean "grow": a judgement that keeps own-label but reduces dependence on it is a clear answer.',
 reportHead:'What a strong answer weighs up',
 model:`Fernhill should continue to make own-label meals, at least while real incomes are falling. At 55% of revenue, stopping would leave the factory half empty and push up the unit cost of every Fernhill Kitchen meal too. With an income elasticity of −0.4, own-label demand should also rise next year while Fernhill Kitchen's falls. The problem is the supermarkets' power: they review prices every year and can switch suppliers, so the margins are probably thin and Fernhill is very exposed. So the decision depends mainly on how much profit own-label actually makes. Fernhill should keep the contracts for now but aim to reduce how much it relies on them, by growing Fernhill Kitchen when incomes recover.`}),

Object.assign(hw(4),{id:'hw04-15a',type:'e15o',caseId:'kiln',labels:['Stay in the niche market','Move towards the mass market'],
 q:'Kiln & Co Ltd is choosing between:\n• staying in a niche market for premium handmade ceramics\n• moving towards the mass market by having a factory partner make some of its range in larger batches.\nEvaluate these two options and recommend which Kiln & Co Ltd should choose.',
 forP:`Staying in the niche market for premium handmade ceramics plays to what makes Kiln & Co successful. Its mugs sell for £24 because customers value something handmade and different from factory-made ceramics at Dunelm and H&M Home. This is Porter's focus strategy, a differentiated product aimed at a small part of the market. Because customers want this particular product, demand is likely to be price inelastic, so Kiln & Co can keep a high price and a high profit margin on each item. The strategy is working: sales grew by 28.1% between 2024 and 2025, more than twice the 12.3% growth of the online handmade homeware market. Staying niche also avoids competing with large retailers that have much lower costs from economies of scale. Kiln & Co would keep control of quality, which matters after its early problems with cracked glazes, and protect the brand that customers are paying for.`,
 againstP:`Moving towards the mass market with a factory partner would let Kiln & Co sell far more than four employees can make by hand. A factory making larger batches would have lower unit costs from economies of scale, so Kiln & Co could sell at lower prices and reach shoppers who would never pay £24 for a mug. This is market development in Ansoff's Matrix, taking the designs to a much bigger group of customers. Kiln & Co's sales of £296,000 are tiny compared with the £410 million online handmade homeware market, let alone the whole homeware market, so the potential growth is large. Priya's skills are in design and marketing, and a factory partner would free her to focus on these instead of production. If only part of the range is factory-made, Kiln & Co could keep its handmade pieces as a premium line while the cheaper range brings in much higher revenue.`,
 depends:['whether customers would still see Kiln & Co as special if some of its range is factory-made','whether Kiln & Co could compete on price with Dunelm and H&M Home, which have much bigger economies of scale','how much growth Priya wants, and whether handmade production can keep up with demand','whether the factory partner could keep quality high'],
 report:'A strong recommendation is about Kiln & Co’s position: its £24 mugs only sell because they are handmade and different. The key weighing is whether a factory-made range would grow sales or damage the brand, and whether Kiln & Co could compete on price with large retailers.',
 reportHead:'What a strong answer weighs up',
 model:`I recommend Kiln & Co stays in the niche market. Its whole advantage is that customers will pay £24 for a mug because it is handmade, and that is working: sales grew 28.1% last year against 12.3% for the market. Moving towards the mass market would mean competing with Dunelm and H&M Home, which have far bigger economies of scale, so Kiln & Co would be fighting on price with no cost advantage. It could also make customers wonder what they are paying extra for. The strongest argument for the factory partner is capacity, since four people can only make so much. So the decision depends mainly on whether handmade production can keep up with demand. If it can't, Priya should grow it carefully, for example by taking on more potters, rather than switch to factory-made products that weaken the brand.`}),

Object.assign(hw(4),{id:'hw04-15b',type:'e15w',caseId:'brightwave',labels:['More promotion','New series of figures'],
 q:'Brightwave Toys Ltd wants to increase sales of Pocket Pals.\nEvaluate two ways Brightwave Toys Ltd could do this and recommend which is most suitable.\nCalculate the size of the collectable figures market in 2025 and use it in your answer.',
 calc:'Market size = £0.9m ÷ 3% = £0.9m ÷ 0.03 = £30 million. The largest rival’s 30% share is about £9 million. Each 1% of market share is worth £0.3 million.',
 forP:`One way to increase sales of Pocket Pals is to spend more on promotion aimed at children, such as YouTube unboxing videos and social media campaigns. The collectable figures market is £0.9m ÷ 0.03 = £30 million, and Pocket Pals has only 3% of it, against 30% (about £9 million) for the largest rival. Every extra 1% of market share is worth £300,000 of sales. Collectables depend on children knowing about them and wanting to complete a set, so promotion that gets Pocket Pals talked about in playgrounds could raise sales quickly. In the Boston Matrix Pocket Pals is a question mark, with low share in a market growing at 15%, and Brightwave can fund the campaign from Stackers, its cash cow. More promotion is market penetration in Ansoff's Matrix, selling the existing product to the existing market, which is the lowest-risk way to grow.`,
 againstP:`Another way is to bring out a new series of figures each season, with some limited editions. Children buy collectables because there is always something new to collect, so a new series gives existing fans a reason to keep buying and gives new customers a reason to start. This is product development in Ansoff's Matrix. It also tackles the real problem. The market is worth £30 million and growing at 15%, so customers are buying collectables, but Pocket Pals has only 3% of the market, which suggests children prefer what rivals offer. Promotion alone would push a product that is not winning, whereas new figures make the product itself more appealing. Limited editions create scarcity, so demand is less price sensitive and Brightwave can avoid discounting. The largest rival's 30% share means rivalry is strong (Porter's Five Forces), so Pocket Pals needs something different to stand out rather than just more adverts.`,
 depends:['why Pocket Pals has only 3% of the market: too few children have heard of it, or they prefer rivals’ figures','how much Brightwave can spend, given it is also launching Bolt','how quickly new figures can be designed and made compared with launching a campaign','how the largest rival, with about £9m of sales, responds'],
 report:'The market is £0.9m ÷ 0.03 = £30 million, growing at 15%, with Pocket Pals at 3% and the largest rival at 30%. A strong recommendation uses these figures to ask why Pocket Pals is so far behind, and chooses the way that fixes that reason.',
 reportHead:'What a strong answer weighs up',
 model:`I recommend bringing out a new series of figures. The collectable figures market is worth £30 million and growing at 15%, so children are clearly buying collectables, but Pocket Pals has only 3% while the largest rival has 30%. That suggests the main problem is the product, not that nobody has heard of it. More promotion would put Pocket Pals in front of more children, but if they prefer rivals' figures it will not turn into many sales. New figures and limited editions give children a reason to choose Pocket Pals and keep collecting. The decision depends mainly on why sales are low, which Brightwave should check with research among children. It also depends on money, as Bolt is launching too. If awareness turns out to be the problem, promotion would be the cheaper fix, but on the evidence here a new series is more likely to increase sales.`})
];

/* models and theories from the 7138 specification, for the check below */
const MODELS=[['Porter',/Porter/],['Ansoff',/Ansoff/],['Boston Matrix',/Boston Matrix|cash cow|question mark|\bdog\b|\bstar\b/i],['Product life cycle',/life cycle|maturity stage|decline stage|extension strateg/i],
  ['PED',/price elasticity|\bPED\b|price (in)?elastic/i],['YED',/income elasticity|\bYED\b/i],['Stakeholder mapping',/stakeholder mapping/i],['Carroll',/Carroll/],['Market mapping',/market mapping/i],
  ['Opportunity cost',/opportunity cost/i],['Economies of scale',/economies of scale/i],['Return on marketing spend',/return on marketing spend/i],['Dividend yield',/dividend yield/i],['Confidence interval',/confidence (level|interval)/i],
  ['Contribution',/contribution/i],['Market share',/market share/i]];
function modelCheck(){
  const rows=[];Q.forEach(q=>[['forP','Paragraph 1'],['againstP','Paragraph 2']].forEach(([k,lab])=>{const found=MODELS.filter(([,re])=>re.test(q[k])).map(([n])=>n);rows.push({id:q.id,para:lab,models:found.join(', ')||'NONE'});}));
  return rows;
}

const byId=Object.fromEntries(Q.map(q=>[q.id,q]));
window.CBANK={TYPES,Q,byId,HW_DUE,modelCheck};
})();
